[{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_000626","candidate_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_000626","source_record_id":"rec_000626","source_corpus":"include","title":"Probing genomic language models: Nucleotide Generative Pretrained Transformer and the role of pretraining in learned representations","abstract":"The value and nature of the representations learned during the pretraining of genomic language models (gLMs) remain actively debated. We introduce Nucleotide Generative Pretrained Transformer (GPT), a decoder-only transformer with single-nucleotide tokenization, to dissect the role of pretraining. Through experiments varying repetitive element (RE) weights during pretraining (0.0 -1.0), comparative finetuning against random initialization, linear probing of internal representations, and sparse autoencoder (SAE)-based interpretability, we evaluated the impact of pretraining and how REs in genomic data influence model learning. Models with moderate RE downweighting (0.5) consistently achieved optimal performance across seven genomic classification tasks, with pretrained models providing substantial performance gains over baselines. SAE feature annotation via sequence alignment revealed substantial RE-associated patterns in the pretrained model internal representations, suggesting that REs -which comprise 30% -60% of mammalian genomes -may dominate the pretraining objective. Our findings support the utility of pretraining and underscore the need for pretraining strategies that better accommodate repetitive sequences across the genome while also fostering the learning of less common but biologically important representations. This study highlights a key challenge for gLMs: ensuring that models broadly learn functional genomic syntax beyond simply recognizing ubiquitous repeats. [truncated]","doi":"10.1093/bib/bbag011","year":"","venue":"","sources":[],"full_text_context":"[abstract: Abstract]\nThe value and nature of the representations learned during the pretraining of genomic language models (gLMs) remain actively debated. We introduce Nucleotide Generative Pretrained Transformer (GPT), a decoder-only transformer with single-nucleotide tokenization, to dissect the role of pretraining. Through experiments varying repetitive element (RE) weights during pretraining (0.0 -1.0), comparative finetuning against random initialization, linear probing of internal representations, and sparse autoencoder (SAE)-based interpretability, we evaluated the impact of pretraining and how REs in genomic data influence model learning. Models with moderate RE downweighting (0.5) consistently achieved optimal performance across seven genomic classification tasks, with pretrained models providing substantial performance gains over baselines. SAE feature annotation via sequence alignment revealed substantial RE-associated patterns in the pretrained model internal representations, suggesting that REs -which comprise 30% -60% of mammalian genomes -may dominate the pretraining objective. Our findings support the utility of pretraining and underscore the need for pretraining strategies that better accommodate repetitive sequences across the genome while also fostering the learning of less common but biologically important representations. This study highlights a key challenge for gLMs: ensuring that models broadly learn functional genomic syntax beyond simply recognizing ubiquitous repeats. [truncated]\n\n[discussion_conclusion: Discussion]\nPretraining Nucleotide GPT with moderate downweighting of REs yielded broadly generalizable representations that significantly improved downstream task performance. The optimal performance achieved with 0.5 RE weighting across all seven classification benchmarks suggests that neither complete exclusion nor full inclusion of these abundant sequences produces the best representations. Models with this balanced weighting outperformed both random initialization and linear baselines, with striking gains on non-TATA promoters (14% improvement over random initialization) and coding versus intergenic classification (16% over linear baseline). This benefit was further supported when linear classifiers were applied to frozen pretrained activations, which achieved 90.0% accuracy on coding versus intergenic sequences -nearly matching the fully finetuned model and substantially exceeding the 84.2% achieved by probing randomly initialized models. Extended layer-wise probing and inter-layer correlation analysis revealed that pretrained and finetuned models develop representations that stabilize around layer 4 and substantially outperform random initialization from that point onward, while random models show fl at performance and uniformly high inter-layer correlations across all depths. These results demonstrate that appropriate pretraining effectively organizes the internal representation space in a task-relevant manner even before supervised fi netuning.\n\nThe choice of tokenization has significant implications for model performance and interpretation. [truncated]\n\n[data_representation: Datasets]\nFor pretraining, Nucleotide GPT was trained on the human reference genome (GRCh38), comprising ∼ 3.1 billion nucleotides. The genome was divided into non-overlapping 8192 bp sequences for model input. REs in the genome were identified using RepeatMasker annotations to enable weighted loss experiments.\n\nWe evaluated Nucleotide GPT on seven benchmark datasets from the Genomic Benchmarks collection (accessible via the Genomic Benchmarks repository bmcgenomdata.biomedcentral.com): (i) Coding Versus Intergenic-binary classification of protein-coding versus intergenic sequences, (ii) Ensembl Regulatory-multi-class classification of promoters, enhancers, and OCRs, (iii) Non-TATA Promoters-binary classification of human non-TATA promoter sequences, (iv) Ensembl Enhancers-binary classification of enhancer sequences, (v) Cohn Enhancers-binary classification of enhancer sequences from an alternative dataset, (vi) Ensembl OCRs-binary classification of OCRs, and (vii) Human or worm-species classification between human and Caenorhabditis elegans sequences. Each dataset is supplied with predefined training and test splits. All input sequences c onsist of standard DNA nucleotides (A, C, G, T).\n\nThe datasets used for fi netuning are derived entirely from genomic sequences, with both positive and negative samples extracted from reference genomes. For human datasets, positive samples represent annotated functional elements (promoters, enhancers, open chromatin regions) from databases such as Ensembl Regulatory Build, FANTOM5, and EPD. [truncated]\n\n[data_representation: Methods]\n#### Datasets\n\nFor pretraining, Nucleotide GPT was trained on the human reference genome (GRCh38), comprising ∼ 3.1 billion nucleotides. The genome was divided into non-overlapping 8192 bp sequences for model input. REs in the genome were identified using RepeatMasker annotations to enable weighted loss experiments.\n\nWe evaluated Nucleotide GPT on seven benchmark datasets from the Genomic Benchmarks collection (accessible via the Genomic Benchmarks repository bmcgenomdata.biomedcentral.com): (i) Coding Versus Intergenic-binary classification of protein-coding versus intergenic sequences, (ii) Ensembl Regulatory-multi-class classification of promoters, enhancers, and OCRs, (iii) Non-TATA Promoters-binary classification of human non-TATA promoter sequences, (iv) Ensembl Enhancers-binary classification of enhancer sequences, (v) Cohn Enhancers-binary classification of enhancer sequences from an alternative dataset, (vi) Ensembl OCRs-binary classification of OCRs, and (vii) Human or worm-species classification between human and Caenorhabditis elegans sequences. Each dataset is supplied with predefined training and test splits. All input sequences c onsist of standard DNA nucleotides (A, C, G, T).\n\nThe datasets used for fi netuning are derived entirely from genomic sequences, with both positive and negative samples extracted from reference genomes. For human datasets, positive samples represent annotated functional elements (promoters, enhancers, open chromatin regions) from databases such as Ensembl Regulatory Build, FANTOM5, and EPD. [truncated]","section_evidence":[{"section_type":"abstract","heading":"Abstract","heading_level":3,"line_start":13,"line_end":38,"text":"The value and nature of the representations learned during the pretraining of genomic language models (gLMs) remain actively debated. We introduce Nucleotide Generative Pretrained Transformer (GPT), a decoder-only transformer with single-nucleotide tokenization, to dissect the role of pretraining. Through experiments varying repetitive element (RE) weights during pretraining (0.0 -1.0), comparative finetuning against random initialization, linear probing of internal representations, and sparse autoencoder (SAE)-based interpretability, we evaluated the impact of pretraining and how REs in genomic data influence model learning. Models with moderate RE downweighting (0.5) consistently achieved optimal performance across seven genomic classification tasks, with pretrained models providing substantial performance gains over baselines. SAE feature annotation via sequence alignment revealed substantial RE-associated patterns in the pretrained model internal representations, suggesting that REs -which comprise 30% -60% of mammalian genomes -may dominate the pretraining objective. Our findings support the utility of pretraining and underscore the need for pretraining strategies that better accommodate repetitive sequences across the genome while also fostering the learning of less common but biologically important representations. This study highlights a key challenge for gLMs: ensuring that models broadly learn functional genomic syntax beyond simply recognizing ubiquitous repeats. [truncated]","original_chars":7065},{"section_type":"discussion_conclusion","heading":"Discussion","heading_level":3,"line_